BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Senior's Social : Lavender Sachet Embroidery Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260824T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260824T123000
UID:6a47c56b7e16a03d00e9f50e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn three different embroidery stitches while creating a sim
 ple embroidered lavender flower on a piece of linen. Then sew the embroide
 ry into a small lavender sachet to create your own pleasant lightly scente
 d piece. Along the way\, participants will gain confidence with basic embr
 oidery techniques and hand-sewing skills.
LOCATION:Junction Triangle\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a47c56b7e16a03d00e9f50e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Afternoon Social: Board Games
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T150000
UID:6a5a39e10ed7b4521ed63c62@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Come join our Adult Social Events at Albert Campbell Library! 
 Albert Campbell Adult Social is a series of activities for adults focusing
  on social interaction and various activities. Bring your friends or make 
 new ones while playing classic board games\, cards\, checkers\, chess\,
  Mahjong\, Monopoly\, Puzzle\, Quiddler\, Qwirkle\, Scrabble\, UpWords\,
  and more!\nDuring this program\, you'll also have the opportunity to lea
 rn\, play\, connect\, and make new friends through Mahjong! Open to both b
 eginners and experienced players\, each session includes a brief introduct
 ion followed by friendly gameplay. No experience needed!\nDrop-in.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Small Meeting Room\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5a39e10ed7b4521ed63c62
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Short Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261111T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261111T193000
UID:69de5e272866a5b4884877f2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you interested in joining a book club but are short on tim
 e?  Then join our Short Book Club\, where we read titles that are 250 page
 s or less. Meetings are held on the second Wednesday of each month.\nOur N
 ovember book is\nObasan\nby Joy Kogawa\nSummary: Based on the author's ow
 n experiences\, this award-winning novel was the first to tell the story o
 f the evacuation\, relocation\, and dispersal of Canadian citizens of Japa
 nese ancestry during the Second World War.\nNew members are most welcome! 
 Please inquire at the circulation desk if you would like a copy of the boo
 k.
LOCATION:Parliament Street\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Short Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T193000
UID:69de5e272866a5b4884877f1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you interested in joining a book club but are short on tim
 e?  Then join our Short Book Club\, where we read titles that are 250 page
 s or less. Meetings are held on the second Wednesday of each month.\nOur O
 ctober book is\nThe Call of the Wild\nby Jack London\nSummary: The advent
 ures of an unusual dog\, part St. Bernard\, part Scotch shepherd\, who is 
 forcibly taken to the Klondike gold fields where he eventually becomes the
  leader of a wolf pack. Buck is a dog born to luxury\, but his life change
 s dramatically when he is sold to be a sled dog in the Yukon Terrority. He
  earns a reputation for his strength and courage\, and is rescued from a s
 eries of bad owners by John Thornton\, who teaches him to love. When John 
 is killed\, Buck is free to follow the call of the wild. First published i
 n 1903\, this masterpiece of adventure and survival continues to enthrall 
 readers almost a century later.\nNew members are most welcome! Please inqu
 ire at the circulation desk if you would like a copy of the book.
LOCATION:Parliament Street\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tea and Entertainment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T160000
UID:6a77497e4523092f0035ae61@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy watching DVDs in the Library Auditorium.\nCoffee\, tea a
 nd cookies or snacks will be served after the film. Ingredient lists will 
 be available upon request.\nArt of the Heist: The Man Who Stole the Mona L
 isa\nA bold inside job at the Louvre turns into one of history’s most sh
 ocking art thefts when a seemingly ordinary handyman steals the Mona Lisa 
 and vanishes without a trace. This gripping film reveals how a simple crim
 e made the world’s most famous painting even more legendary. Closed capt
 ions. 55 minutes. .2015
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a77497e4523092f0035ae61
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Bingo
